// REJECTS: Unexpected token '..'. Valid alternatives: [']', '.']
//
// `String[0..1]` in a DeephavenApp function signature. Deephaven writes its own multiplicity
// rule rather than using M3's:
//     appMultiplicity : BRACKET_OPEN (STAR | INTEGER (DOT DOT INTEGER?)?) BRACKET_CLOSE
// -- a range spelled as TWO separate DOT tokens. But this section is lexed with
// CoreLexerGrammar, which lexes `..` as a SINGLE token, so the DOT DOT sequence the rule asks
// for can never be produced. The range arm is unreachable by construction, and only `[n]` and
// `[*]` are typeable.
//
// A grammar bug that a rewrite fixes by accident: transcribe `appMultiplicity` against a lexer
// that does not have a `..` token -- or simply reuse M3's `multiplicity`, which every other
// function signature in Legend uses and which accepts `[0..1]` -- and the rewrite is more
// permissive than legend-engine on the most ordinary multiplicity there is.
//
// The alternative list is the tell: at that position the parser wants `]` or a single `.`,
// and the input has neither because the lexer already glued the pair together.
###Deephaven
DeephavenApp fixture::neg::RangeMultiplicity
{
    applicationName: 'MyTestApp';
    function: fixture::neg::myFunc(String[0..1]):Any[*];
    ownership: Deployment { identifier: 'owner123' };
}
